                  "padding": [
                    22,
                    20
                  ],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "QQK5U",
                      "name": "Mobile Article Author",
                      "fill": "#6B665F",
                      "content": "Maya Chen · 12 August 2026",
                      "fontFamily": "Inter",
                      "fontSize": 10,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "mRN7B",
                      "name": "Mobile Article Dek",
                      "fill": "#6B665F",
                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "content": "Why durable tools often begin with fewer assumptions—and how restraint compounds over time.",
                      "lineHeight": 1.35,
                      "fontFamily": "Newsreader",
                      "fontSize": 16,
                      "fontWeight": "normal",
                      "fontStyle": "italic"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "rectangle",
                      "id": "I2NoPG",
                      "name": "Mobile Article Rule",
                      "fill": "#E5E0D8",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "height": 1
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "U7WyR",
                      "name": "Mobile Article Body",
                      "fill": "#171512",
                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "content": "Complex systems are usually built one decision at a time. Each addition feels locally reasonable: another setting, another status, another escape hatch. Yet the systems we return to are often the ones that ask less of us.\n\nSimplicity is not the absence of capability. It is the result of arranging capability so the common path remains obvious.\n\nPreserve the record first, then improve it. Background work may enrich what was saved, but should never determine whether it exists.",
                      "lineHeight": 1.45,
                      "fontFamily": "Newsreader",
                      "fontSize": 15,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
